/*
 * **Pre-engineered module variants** — the outfitting rows you buy already engineered,
 * each mapped to the base module symbol it fits as and the blueprint baked into it.
 *
 * Sources: the in-game outfitting and blueprint registries, cross-checked against
 *          Inara (https://inara.cz/elite/outfitting/, https://inara.cz/elite/blueprints/)
 *          and Frontier's update notes for the release that introduced each variant.
 *          The community-goal and tech-broker rewards come from EDSY's stored-module
 *          presets (github.com/taleden/EDSY), (c) taleden under a CC BY-NC 4.0 License
 *          (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/).
 *          The final-engineering restriction on the seven Guardian-weapon rows is an
 *          in-game report from the repository maintainer, observed 2026-08-09 UTC.
 *          The underlying values are Elite Dangerous game data, property of Frontier
 *          Developments plc, redistributed under Frontier's media-usage terms:
 *          https://forums.frontier.co.uk/threads/510879/
 *
 * A pre-engineered module has **no symbol of its own**: the game sells or awards an
 * ordinary module with engineering already applied, and a journal `Loadout` reports it
 * as the base `symbol` plus an `Engineering` block. So each record here is a *pairing*,
 * not a new module — `symbol` joins to the module catalogues, `blueprint` to
 * `blueprints.jsonc`, the optional `experimental` to `experimental-effects.jsonc`, and
 * `grade` is the grade already applied.
 *
 * `acquisition` says where the variant comes from:
 *   - `"mercenary"`     — the Merc-Coin shop rows, all at grade 1.
 *   - `"communityGoal"` — awarded for taking part in a community goal. Mostly grade 5,
 *     and often carrying an experimental effect the shop rows do not.
 *   - `"techBroker"`    — unlocked at a tech broker. Human brokers stock the "V1" drives
 *     and seeker racks; the Guardian weapon rows come from the Salvation, Azimuth and
 *     Sirius brokers. Several are recorded elsewhere as having *also* been community-goal
 *     rewards; this field records the one route the source states, not every route.
 *
 * `engineeringLocked: true` marks the seven pre-engineered Guardian weapons as final.
 * Their stock counterparts can take Anti-Guardian Zone Resistance; these bought or
 * awarded articles accept no blueprint or experimental effect at all.
 *
 * `mercCoinCost` is the shop price in **Merc Coin**, a separate currency with no credit
 * equivalent — which is why it is its own field rather than the `cost` modules carry.
 * Only the `mercenary` rows have one. Tech-broker unlock costs are not stored: they are
 * paid in materials and commodities, not in any single currency.
 *
 * `modifiers` is the variant's hand-set stat block, in the same vocabulary blueprints
 * and experimental effects use — a journal Modifier `label`, a `method`
 * (`multiplicative` / `additive` / `overwrite`) and a `value`. It is what makes these
 * records fittable: apply them over the base module's stats to get the engineered
 * module. The 22 `mercenary` rows carry none — no registry publishes the grade-1
 * pre-engineering they arrive with, and a guess is worse than an omission.
 *
 * Modifier values are the **authored decimals, recovered — not rounded by eye.** The
 * source encodes them in a custom 20-bit float, which carries about fifteen significant
 * bits, so decoding a change the game states as `+20%` yields `0.199997`. Each value
 * stored here is the *shortest decimal that re-encodes to the identical bits*, so it is
 * the figure the encoder was given, and every one of the 51 stat blocks recovers
 * exactly. Nothing is approximated: a value with no short form would have been left as
 * decoded, and none needed it. The recovery is why the 5A "FSD V1" resolves to a whole
 * 1785 optimal mass from `+0.7` rather than to 1785.0126 from `0.699988`.
 *
 * One modifier is deliberately dropped: burst interval, which 13 variants carry and
 * which Frontier's journal has no Modifier Label for. It drives rate of fire, and
 * deriving a `RateOfFire` value from it needs weapon base stats the module catalogues
 * do not hold — so it is omitted rather than approximated.
 *
 * The Merc recipes start at grade 2 precisely because grade 1 is what the purchased
 * module already contains; `getBlueprintCost(bp, target, 1)` prices taking one of those
 * the rest of the way. Community-goal and tech-broker rewards are mostly already at
 * grade 5 — but not always: the Guardian weapon rows record grade 1, and there the
 * blueprint's own grade 1 exists, so the grade is a real grade of a real recipe. That
 * identity does not make the final Guardian articles engineerable.
 *
 * **A reward variant is not reproducible by engineering the same blueprint.** Its stat
 * block also carries hand-set modifier overrides that no blueprint grants, which is
 * exactly what makes it a reward — the 2A Guardian Shard Cannon, for instance, arrives
 * with an armour-piercing bonus Long Range does not confer. The
 * `blueprint`/`grade`/`experimental` recorded here identify what it *is*, not a recipe
 * that recreates it.
 *
 * No single field is unique, and neither is any triple: the 2A Guardian Shard Cannon
 * carries Long Range with no experimental *twice* — once at grade 1 from the Salvation
 * broker and once at grade 5 as a community-goal reward. The identity of a variant is
 * the (`symbol`, `blueprint`, `grade`, `experimental`) quadruple.
 *
 * Full provenance: data/ships/SOURCES.md.
 */
